Developing inexpensive electrocatalysts with superior catalytic activity and high stability for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) are crucial to the commercialization of fuel cells.[1] Researchers have demonstrated that heteroatom doping, e.g., N, can affect the electronic structure of neighboring carbon atoms and induce the uneven charge distribution of carbon atoms, which subsequently enhance the electrochemical reactivity.[2] However, none of the methods has achieved atomically precisely controlled locations of dopants to enhance the catalytic activities. Recently, the rationally designed metal organic frameworks (MOF)- and covalent organic frameworks (COFs)- based electrocatalysts for oxygen reduction in fuel cells have received extensive attention owning to their tunable structures with desired multi-element composition and high surface area.[1c, 3] For example, Lou et al. combined nitrogen-rich MOF with coreshell inorganic nanocage to prepare many MOF-based composites as efficient electrocatalysts with remarkable electrocatalytic performance for the ORR in alkaline conditions.[4] Feng et al. reported a series of work on growing porous polymers on substrate-supported graphene, in which graphene sheet features good conductivity and a large specific surface area.[5] These resulting hybrids exhibit a large surface area and outstanding electrochemical performance for ORR. Nevertheless. due to the poor conductive properties of MOFs/COFs, their intrinsic T-conjugated structures or enhanced TT-conjugation by pyrolysis are essential for the electrocatalytic activity, which may induce undesirable structure changes and even destruct the fine structures,leavingthemechanismofelectrocatalysis uncertain.[6] Therefore, it is urgent to obtain MOFs/COFs-based electrocatalysts with well-defined structures and controllable active sites without pyrolysis.
Recently, well-defined covalent organic polymers (COPs), a class of COF analogues but with high thermodynamic stability, have been widely developed.[7] Although efforts have been made recentlytosynthesize well-definedCOPs withhigh thermodynamic stability and the potentials of atomically precisely controllable capacities, such as robust tailoring heteroatom incorporation and location of active sites for ORR at the atomic scale,[1c,7a,7c,8] the conductivity of the COPs is usually still poor, leading to low catalytic activities. As a highly conducting 2D material, graphene exhibits large specific surface area, large conjugated system, unique high charge mobility and high thermal/chemical  stability,[9]  which  could  significantly enhances charge transfer from the current collector to active intrinsic COP material.[10]  To overcome the challenge while keeping atomically precisely controllable capabilities，we have developed a new facile approach to synthesize a hybrid material i.e., COP-P- $S O_{3}$ -Co-rGO (termed as 4), by directly combining pristine COP with reduced graphene oxide (rGO） via selfassembly. To obtain rich active sites, we selected porphyrin analogues with the high nitrogen content as monomers to prepare COP-based electrocatalyst.[1c, 8] The as-synthesized hybrid 4 not only exhibits a significant enhancement in ORR activity with a positive half-wave better than that of the intrinsic COP material, but also a good durability in alkaline solutions.
The as-synthesized COP materials have very low solubility in water. To facilitate the self-assembly between COP material and graphene by the attraction, we first prepared a better waterdispersive porphyrin macrocycles-based COP-P (termed as 1) (Table S1) material with sulfonic acid side groups, COP-P- $\cdot\mathsf{s o}_{3}\mathsf{H}$ (termed as 2), via post-synthetic modification according to our previous method (Figure S3 and S4).[11] Moreover, considering the electronegativity of graphene oxide in solution, we further incorporated metal ion (e.g.， Co) into the fully conjugated polymer COP backbone via connecting with sulfonic acid side groups to further accelerate self-assembly between COP material and graphene in the solution. As shown in Figure 1a the electrocatalyst 4 was prepared through non-covalent assembly between the designed COP, i.e., COP-P- ${\cdot}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}$ (termed as 3), and graphene oxide (GO) followed by the reduction of the GO. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images revealed the layered texture of the COP-P and the thin graphene layers (Figures S1a and S1b). The above scheme is supported by the result of the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images of 4 and the following Xray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Figure 1b clearly shows the cross-linking between COP structure and graphene. As demonstrated in Figure 1c, COP-P exhibits the expected amorphous texture and graphene presents the carbon lattice fringes with an interplanar spacing of 0.34 nm (the typical C (0 0
2) plane), agreeing with the results ofXRD(FigureS2).The distribution of elements on hybrid 4 was  revealed by high-angle annular dark-field (HAADF)-STEM images (Figure 1d, $\textsf{e}$ and Figure S5). The energy dispersive X-Ray (EDX) spectroscopy shows that there are obvious sulfur and cobalt peaks in the amorphous region, which can be ascribed to COP material, but only one apparent C peak exists in the ultrathin region, corresponding to graphene (Figure 1d). This reduction was further confirmed by the line scan EDX spectroscopy(Figure1e）and elemental mapping (Figure S5).
XPS was used to probe the nature of the chemical bonding and the elemental composition of 4.The survey XPS spectrum of 4 clearly shows five visible peaks corresponding to carbon (C 1s), oxygen (O 1s), nitrogen (N 1s), sulfur (S 2p) and cobalt (Co 2p) in hybrid 4 (Figure S6). For N1s spectra， the pronounced peak at 399.9 belongs to the pyrrolic N. As can be seen from the highresolution C 1s spectrum (Figure S7a), the peak at 286.8 eV belongs to C-O,whereas the peak located at $289.2\mathsf{e V}$ corresponds to the $c=0$ ，which comes from the partially reduced GO [12] due to the connection between COP and GO. In the high-resolution S 2p spectrum (Figure S6b), the pronounced peaks at 162.1 and $163.3~\mathsf{e V}$ belong to the C-S structure, while the peaks at 167.9 and $169\L\Theta V$ represent $\mathsf{S O}_{3}{}^{2-}$ ,[13] indicating that the S elements have been successfully doped into the COP sheets. As mentioned before, Co is introduced to increase the interactions between the rGO and COP. Here,Co was identified to exist as ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ by the survey spectrum XPS of Co2p for 4 which shows a low-energy band $(\mathsf{C o2p}_{3/2})$ and a highenergy band $(\mathsf{C o2p}_{1/2})$ at 781.1 and $796.6\ \Lt\ \Lt\Theta V,$ respectively (Figure S6c).[14] Moreover, the elemental analysis (Table S2) and inductive coupled plasma (lCP) results (Table S3) of 4 show that the content of S and Co are 2.96 and $2.87~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ， respectively, corresponding to about 2:1 mole ratio for S and Co in hybrid 4, agreeing with the stoichiometric coupling. From the above results along with the electrocatalytic performance (vide infra), it is evidential that hybrid 4 has been successfully synthesized by the self-assembly between COP material and graphene with the aid of ionic attraction between cobalt sulfonate from COP and carbonyl groups from GO (Figure 1a).
The electrocatalytic activities of catalyst 4 were evaluated using a typical three-electrode system in an $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1~\mathsf{M}$ KOH solution. Among the three electrocatalysts, catalyst 4 exhibited the highest ORR catalytic activities with the onset potential of $0.88~\mathsf{V}$ (vs.RHE),which is $200~\mathrm{mV}$ and $100~\mathsf{m V}$ more positive than that of pristine 3 (0.68V vs. RHE) and rGO (0.78V vs. RHE), respectively (Figure 2a). The electric conduction measurements showed that the electrical conductivity of catalysts 4 $(2.56\times10^{-1}\mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{m}^{-1})$ was about $8.4\times10^{7}$ times much higher than that of COP catalyst $(3.06\times10^{-9}\textsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{-1})$ (Table S4), implying that the enhanced ORR activity is attributed to presence of highly conductive rGO. Moreover, the corresponding limiting current density of 4 was also remarkably increased by over two times than that of pristine 3. The electron transfer number of catalyst 4 reached 3.70 at $0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ VS.RHE (Figure 2b). Although this value is slightly lower than that of $\mathsf{P t/C}$ (3.85), it represents a remarkable enhancement compared with that of the single 3 (0.56) or rGO (2.75), suggesting that the ORR transformed from a two-electron transfer reaction to a fourelectron transfer reaction after the COP was combined with rGO to form hybrid 4. The excellent ORR activity of catalyst 4 was further demonstrated by the small Tafel slope of 67.4 mV dec-1 close to $66~\mathsf{m V}$ dec-1 for $\mathsf{P t/C}$ ，indicating a similar reaction mechanism of ORR on the catalyst surface as $\mathsf{P t}$ (Figure $20$ ： Furthermore, the good durability of 4 was supported by cycling continuously for 30oo cycles ranging from 0.6 to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ Vs. RHE at a scan rate of $100\mathrm{~mV~}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ in an $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathrm{~M~}\mathsf{K O H}$ solution. At the end of the cycling, catalyst 4 presents similar linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves with the initial cycle with negligible loss in cathodic current density (Figure 2d). In addition, this remarkable enhancement also can be found in the hybrids with other conductive materials, e.g.， carbon black, carbon nanotube (Figure S8).As shown in Figure S9a, catalyst 4 showed higher anti-methanol poisoning performance than $\mathsf{P t/C}$ .
The improved ORR activities observed in this study for the 4 complex over 3 could be mainly attributed to the synergistic effect of highly conductive graphene and COP with rich active sites via fast intermolecular charge and ion transportation. While rGO has good electrical conductivity, which improves the electric conductivity of composites materials and can provide a highly conductive pathway for electrons transfer, the COP can provide highly efficient ORR active sites.
It should be mentioned that cobalt species in this hybrid are the bridge to connect COP and graphene,which facilitates the self-assembly but unlikely contributes to the ORR activity. As shown $\mathrm{in}$ Figure 2e, after introducing SCN- into the alkaline electrolyte( $10~\mathsf{m M})$ , the onset potential and the current density of catalyst 4 exhibit negligible changes, which indicates that cobalt ion $(\mathsf{C o}^{2+})$ in the 4 backbone was not the active sites for ORR. In addition, compared with rGO, the ORR performance of the Co incorporated rGO sample (rGO-Co) exhibits as similar as the unmodified rGO, suggesting that Co species are not main active sites (Figure S1O). In order to further confirm this conclusion, X-rayabsorptionfinestructure(XAFS) measurements were carried out to investigate the structure of Co species at the atomic level. The Fourier-transformed (FT) k3- weighted extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectrum of 4 in Figure 3a shows only one main peak at1.50 A which is assigned to the $\mathsf{C o-O}$ distance, and no
